{"name":"Eye of the Storm","hex":"#d9e3d9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eye of the storm","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A region of calm weather right in the middle of a storm.","A period of calm in a tumultuous time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eye_of_the_storm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eye (cyclone)","blocks":[{"text":"The eye is a region of mostly calm weather at the center of a tropical cyclone. The eye of a storm is a roughly circular area, typically 30–65 kilometers in diameter. It is surrounded by the eyewall, a ring of towering thunderstorms where the most severe weather and highest winds of the cyclone occur. The cyclone's lowest barometric pressure occurs in the eye and can be as much as 15 percent lower than the pressure outside the storm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg/330px-Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_(cyclone)"},{"title":"The Eye of the Storm (1970 film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Eye of the Storm is a 1970 American television documentary featuring schoolteacher Jane Elliott conducting her \"Blue Eyes/Brown Eyes\" exercise in discrimination, in her third-grade classroom at Riceville Elementary School in Riceville, Iowa. Riceville, a small town near the Minnesota border, is almost entirely white; in that context, young children had little understanding of the concept of discrimination. The documentary is narrated by Bill Beutel and directed by William Peters, who developed the idea with his spouse and associate producer, Muriel Peters."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Eye_of_the_Storm_(1970_film)"},{"title":"Eye of the Storm (One Ok Rock album)","blocks":[{"text":"Eye of the Storm is the ninth studio album by Japanese rock band One Ok Rock. It was released on February 13, 2019, in Japan through A-Sketch, and on February 15, 2019, in other countries through Fueled by Ramen. The album marked a shift in their sound from the emo and alternative rock of their previous albums to pop and electronic music. Three singles, \"Change\", \"Stand Out Fit In\", and \"Wasted Nights\" have been released in support of the album."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3c/One_Ok_Rock_%E2%80%93_Eye_of_the_Storm.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_the_Storm_(One_Ok_Rock_album)"},{"title":"Eye","blocks":[{"text":"An eye is a sensory organ that allows an organism to perceive visual information. It detects light and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Krilleyekils.jpg/330px-Krilleyekils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg/500px-Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg/500px-Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKate_2003-10-04_1420Z_(alternate).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Winnie_1997-08-17_0600Z_%28landscape%29.png/500px-Winnie_1997-08-17_0600Z_%28landscape%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Winnie_1997-08-17_0600Z_%28landscape%29.png/500px-Winnie_1997-08-17_0600Z_%28landscape%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typhoon Winnie is tied with Typhoon Carmen for the largest eye on record at 200 miles (320 km).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWinnie_1997-08-17_0600Z_(landscape).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg/500px-Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg/1280px-Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg/500px-Wilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hurricane Wilma with a pinhole eye","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilma_2005-10-19_1640Z.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/KTBW_loop_of_Ian%27s_First_Southwest_Florida_Landfall_9-28-2022.gif/500px-KTBW_loop_of_Ian%27s_First_Southwest_Florida_Landfall_9-28-2022.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/KTBW_loop_of_Ian%27s_First_Southwest_Florida_Landfall_9-28-2022.gif/500px-KTBW_loop_of_Ian%27s_First_Southwest_Florida_Landfall_9-28-2022.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typically, eyes are easy to spot using weather radar. This radar image of Hurricane Ian clearly shows the eye near Fort Myers, Florida.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKTBW_loop_of_Ian's_First_Southwest_Florida_Landfall_9-28-2022.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Emnati_2022-02-21_0955Z.jpg/500px-Emnati_2022-02-21_0955Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https: